      "createdAt": "2026-10-14T09:54:23Z",
      "storyText": "Been recently thinking about this more and more: When the need for a human in the design loop ceases?<p>Given that LLMs are RL&#x27;d to the max with everything that can be given a wrong&#x2F;right answer (code&#x2F;maths), what currently is stopping Anthropic, OpenAI et al. from internally building (and maybe buying) datasets of good and bad architecture, and RL&#x27;ing that?<p>Given that LLMs were a fancy autocomplete back in 2024, and now people are talking about building &quot;software factories&quot; with the agents, what in your mind is the stop to this development? Why software architecture would be hard for agents still in a year or two?<p>Just thinking because I see more and more examples of software developers ignoring this and saying &quot;humans still have a place in guiding the agents and designing the architecture&quot;. I think (businesswise) the need for actual software programmer skills is 90% out in a few years I think, and product lead etc people will be favored more as they have more people skills.<p>it was a good run.",
